[Stale Guard Counter] (sim-review): 0x1fd07693355ac7f1c29d2fc2bbc390fc0d5ad2ee's guard check at pc 1967 reads storage slot 0x4, which held the exact same value (0x16345785d8a0000) across 6 separate successful calls through this guard. If this slot is meant to be a counter or usage total that a `require(slot < LIMIT)`-style check enforces a cap against, a value that never moves despite repeated successful activity usually means the increment logic is missing, unreachable, or gated behind something unrelated -- making the advertised limit silently ineffective (confirmed in a real deployed contract this session: a token-mint cap whose counter's increment had been commented out). Review item, not a certain bug: a guard CAN legitimately read a slot that stays constant by design (an admin flag, a one-time init check) -- worth a manual look at what this slot is supposed to track and whether anything actually updates it.

